        "reading": "every control runs the identical GP protocol on 2k = 22 coordinates and changes only how the 55x11 block is chosen. The candidate beats a random block by 9.7x, a row-permutation of its own eigenvectors by 10.9x and the shuffled-phase gather by 12.8x. The shuffled-phase control is the one the original request names: when the loads' phase labels are permuted the construction loses most of its advantage, so the phase labels carry real information and the phase-aware claim is supported rather than incidental. The hop-count graph Laplacian and the injection PCA both land near the mean predictor, so neither graph structure without impedance nor generic compression reproduces it."
      },
      "decomposition": {
        "file": "results/decomposition_n80.json",
        "available": true,
        "mean_ols": {
          "spectral": 0.09245997085096236,
          "pca": 0.9343906561385508,
          "random": 0.2585305594005782
        },
        "mean_gp": {
          "spectral": 0.02216952685513428,
          "pca": 0.9311452973649451,
          "random": 0.2578807283688833
        },
        "learner_gain": {
          "spectral": 4.1705883691220675,
          "pca": 1.0034853408837372,
          "random": 1.002519889856854
        },
        "spectrum_gain_at_gp": 42.00113531738723,
        "spectrum_gain_at_ols": 10.10589390780481,
        "reading": "the wide-RBF GP at the selected lengthscale is a nonlinear interpolator. It improves on OLS by 4.17x on the spectral coordinates but by only 1.00x on PCA coordinates, so the learner is not the source of the margin by itself: it amplifies a signal that is already present. At the same learner the spectral coordinates are 42.0x better than the 22 PCA coordinates, and 10.1x better at the linear learner. The margin therefore traces to the feeder's own Laplacian spectrum, and the honest caveat is that the wide-RBF kernel is what converts a 10x coordinate advantage into a 40x error advantage, so both halves of the construction are load-bearing."
      }
